INT. ROOFTOP BAR, HOTEL TERRACE - NIGHT

Candles in glass chimneys. A city of lights twenty floors down. Jazz from a speaker. The bar is nearly empty. A velvet rope hangs from brass posts near the stairwell door.

A STRANGER in a trench coat sits alone at the end of the bar, a half-finished martini before him. His hat is pulled low. On his sleeve is a small spot of dried blood.

VIVIENNE ASHFORD-LANE, thirty-five, in an emerald gown and long gloves, enters through the stairwell door. She pauses. She crosses to the bar and takes the stool beside his. The BARTENDER, DOMINIC, fifty, polishes a glass with a white cloth.

VIVIENNE
Whisky. Neat.

DOMINIC
Right away, Miss Ashford-Lane.

The stranger turns his head. He studies her. She does not meet his gaze.

STRANGER
You come here often?

VIVIENNE
Every Thursday.

STRANGER
Then you'll know the barman's been paid to forget me.

Dominic's polishing stops. The glass rings against the bar top.

VIVIENNE
(quietly)
Who are you?

STRANGER
Call me Quentin.

QUENTIN
(lifting his martini)
I'm the man who knows what your husband did on Tuesday.

She sets down her glove. Her pulse jumps in her throat. She reaches for her purse. Quentin's hand closes over her wrist, not roughly.

QUENTIN (CONT'D)
Don't. Dominic has a gun under the register, and I have a better one.

DOMINIC
(softly)
Miss Ashford-Lane, I'm sorry.

VIVIENNE
You work for him?

DOMINIC
For both of you. Different days.

Quentin releases her wrist. He sets a folded envelope on the bar and slides it across.

QUENTIN
Open it.

She does. A single photograph. Her husband, unmoving, at the foot of a staircase. She exhales. For a long moment she says nothing.

VIVIENNE
Thank you.

QUENTIN
That's it?

VIVIENNE
That's it.

She finishes her whisky. She leaves a very large tip.
